"""Original concept lessons; exact syllabus order. Traces are author-created examples."""
LESSONS={}
def put(n,raw):
    LESSONS[n]=[]
    for block in raw.strip().split('\n\n'):
        prose,trace=block.strip().split(' || ')
        LESSONS[n].append(dict(prose=prose,trace=trace.split(' / ')))

put(2, '''A request to build a feed hides several products: reading posts, publishing, moderating, and following. Observe a user journey and distinguish mandatory outcomes from negotiable targets. “Publish once, show followers within five seconds” is actionable; “real-time social app” is not. Prioritize by business harm and uncertainty. Prototype the riskiest assumption first, and record what is excluded: direct messages do not belong in the estimate until explicitly included. || Journey: publish a post / Must: retain accepted post / Target: visible within 5 s / Exclude: direct messages

Users do not generate a constant stream of identical requests. Break a session into operations and attach payload size, arrival rate, fan-out, and dependency cost to each. In an assumed session, opening a feed causes one feed request, twenty image reads, and one analytics event. Counting only the first request underestimates network work. A journey model helps predict which component saturates; production telemetry should replace its assumptions as soon as evidence exists. || 1 feed request / 20 image requests / 1 analytics event / Separate workloads and budgets

A read/write ratio is meaningful only at a named boundary. Ten feed reads per post can cause thousands of writes if publishing materializes follower feeds. Conversely, fan-out-on-read shifts work into read-time joins. Count logical business actions separately from physical operations and amplification. Read replicas help appropriate reads, but cannot eliminate a serialized writer or guarantee read-your-writes when replicas lag. Choose the implementation after identifying the actual pressure. || 1 publish action / 1 durable post write / 1000 follower-feed writes / Logical ratio differs from storage ratio

Latency measures elapsed time; throughput measures completed work per time interval. Offered requests per second can exceed successful completions per second while a queue grows. QPS usually counts queries, RPS requests, and TPS transactions, but teams must define these denominators. At 200 offered requests/s and 180 completions/s, backlog grows by twenty requests each second unless requests are rejected. Reporting only accepted traffic conceals overload and abandoned work. || Offered: 200 requests/s / Completed: 180 requests/s / Backlog: +20 requests/s / Measure rejection and timeout too

A mean can hide users who wait much longer. For 100 illustrative requests, 99 at 10 ms and one at 1000 ms, the mean is 19.9 ms; the slow request is still a second. Percentiles describe the distribution under a specified estimation convention. Do not average machine p99 values to obtain fleet p99; merge distributions or appropriate histograms. Include errors and timed-out operations in the experience model rather than quietly dropping them. || 99 requests at 10 ms / 1 request at 1000 ms / Mean = 19.9 ms / Tail experience remains 1000 ms

A reachable service can serve stale or lost data. Availability is useful response delivery, durability is retention under a failure model, reliability is correct service over time under a stated definition, and consistency constrains allowed observations. A payment status page may remain available while returning an outdated state. Decide which property each operation needs; an inventory decrement and a popularity counter often justify different consistency and latency costs. || Request responds successfully / Response may be stale / Acknowledged state may be lost / Test each promise separately

Choose a user-visible SLI, an objective and window, then describe any contractual SLA separately. A 99.9% event-based success objective across one million eligible requests allows 1000 bad events. Excluding failures by definition invalidates the budget. Error budgets inform release and reliability decisions; they do not authorize spending all failures in one outage. A time-based SLO uses time rather than request count and gives a different allowance. || 1000000 eligible events / Target: 99.9% good / Allowance: 1000 bad events / Track rate of budget consumption

Plan for growth with scenarios rather than a single extrapolation. Separate stored data, working set, background jobs, failure headroom, and migration overlap. An assumed 10% monthly growth gives 1.1^12, about 3.14 times the starting volume after a year. It does not prove traffic or CPU grows at the same rate. A capacity plan should name its limiting resource, procurement or scaling delay, and the measurement that triggers an update. || Starting volume: 1 unit / Monthly multiplier: 1.1 / After 12 months: 3.14 units / Recheck traffic and resource mix

Assume ten million daily users, fifty API requests each, one 2 kB post per user daily, and ten 200 kB images viewed each. Average API load is 500 million/86400, about 5787 requests/s. Raw post growth is 20 GB/day using decimal units; image transfer is 20 TB/day before caching. Replication, indexes, metadata, compression, and retention are separate factors. These are workload calculations, not claims about any company's infrastructure. || API: 500 million requests/day / Posts: 20 GB raw/day / Images: 20 TB delivered/day / Add indexes, replicas and headroom

Average traffic hides synchronized arrivals. With an assumed peak factor of five, the preceding 5787 requests/s becomes about 28935 at peak. A uniform fivefold multiplier is only a planning hypothesis: image and write peaks may differ, and retries can amplify outages. Evaluate a spike's duration as well as height because an autoscaler that starts new instances after the spike may provide little protection. Admission limits must work before scaling catches up. || Average: 5787 requests/s / Assumed peak multiplier: 5 / Peak: 28935 requests/s / Scaling delay still needs protection

Little's Law relates long-run averages across the same stable boundary: L = lambda W. At 500 successful operations/s and 40 ms average residence time, average in-flight work is twenty. If residence time becomes 400 ms at the same stable throughput, it becomes two hundred. The equation is an accounting relationship, not a p99 model or proof that an overloaded queue will stabilize. Rejections, retries, and work outside the boundary need explicit accounting. || Arrival/completion rate: 500/s / Mean residence: 0.04 s / Mean work in flight: 20 / At 0.4 s: 200 in flight

Amdahl's Law bounds ideal fixed-work speedup when only part of the work can be parallelized. If 80% parallelizes perfectly, four workers give 1/(0.2+0.8/4)=2.5 times speedup, while infinitely many approach five. Coordination and changed cache behavior can make real speedup worse. Optimize the actual limiting stage: doubling web workers is ineffective when every request waits for a serialized database operation. Re-measure after each improvement because the bottleneck moves. || Serial fraction: 0.2 / Parallel fraction: 0.8 / 4 workers: 2.5x ideal speedup / Infinite workers: 5x ceiling

A request's tail can be dominated by the slowest dependency. If it waits for all fifty independent calls and each has a 1% chance of exceeding a threshold, the chance that at least one exceeds it is 1−0.99^50, about 39.5%. Independence is an assumption; shared failures can correlate tails. Allocate an end-to-end deadline across network, queues, execution, and response. Hedging may reduce some tails but spends extra capacity and requires safe duplicate handling. || 50 required parallel calls / Per-call slow probability: 0.01 / All fast probability: 0.99^50 / At least one slow: about 39.5%

Capacity is not the only constraint. A cheap design that violates tenant isolation is infeasible; a high-throughput design requiring unsupported operational expertise may be a poor fit. Classify constraints as mandatory and preferences as negotiable. Include staffing, incident recovery, migration, security review, and maintenance in total cost. A vendor-neutral comparison should model the same workload and service promises for each candidate; comparing unlike failure guarantees makes the apparent price advantage misleading. || Check mandatory correctness / Check security and operation limits / Estimate total lifecycle cost / Compare feasible candidates

A decision matrix makes judgments visible but does not turn them into measurements. Apply hard constraints first, then score candidates against defined criteria with explicit weights and uncertainty. If a small weight change reverses the winner, gather more evidence or prefer a reversible choice. Record alternatives and revisit triggers in an ADR. One weighted total cannot establish an invariant, eliminate an unknown dependency, or substitute for a representative load test. || Reject infeasible candidates / Score explicit preferences / Vary uncertain weights / Record choice and revisit trigger''')

put(3, '''Entering a URL begins a chain involving name resolution, a route, transport, a secure application exchange, server execution, and rendering. Each stage may reuse cached state or an existing connection. Trace the actual path before assigning a “network latency” number. A fast server can still deliver a slow page if DNS, connection setup, large assets, or rendering dominate. The application perspective is a set of dependent waits and transfers rather than one instantaneous remote call. || URL parsed / Name and route resolved / Secure connection used / Response parsed and rendered

An IP address identifies a network endpoint within a routing context; a port identifies a transport endpoint at that host. A socket represents a communication endpoint managed by the operating system. Routing forwards packets toward destinations, potentially through several networks and translations. A service listening on port 443 is not necessarily reachable: binding, routing, firewall policy, and return paths all matter. Ports are not authentication credentials, and private address space is not an authorization boundary. || Destination name to address / Route packets toward host / Transport selects port 443 / Application accepts a connection

TCP supplies an ordered reliable byte stream; it preserves bytes, not application-message boundaries. UDP sends datagrams without those stream guarantees. QUIC builds secure streams and transport behavior over UDP, including stream-level loss isolation and connection identifiers. Choose by application requirements and deployed infrastructure, not by claiming UDP is always faster. A lost TCP packet can delay later bytes on the connection; QUIC separates streams but still shares congestion and application dependencies. || Application writes messages / TCP exposes ordered bytes / UDP exposes datagrams / QUIC exposes secure streams

A resolver obtains records for a name, often through caches and authoritative servers. TTL limits intended cache lifetime, not an instantaneous global invalidation deadline. Negative answers can also be cached. During a failover, clients with cached addresses may continue contacting an old endpoint, and long-lived connections may bypass DNS entirely. Test resolver behavior and connection lifetimes together; changing a DNS record alone cannot guarantee immediate traffic movement. || Name lookup / Cached answer valid until expiry / Record changes at authority / Existing clients may retain old route

TLS authenticates peers according to the configured trust model and protects traffic integrity and confidentiality. HTTPS is HTTP over a secure transport arrangement. Certificate validation, hostname checks, key protection, and termination placement are architectural decisions. TLS does not authorize a user to read another tenant's data. If an edge proxy terminates encryption, the next hop needs its own trust and transport policy; a lock icon does not describe every internal connection. || Client validates server identity / Peers establish traffic keys / Encrypted application records / Application still checks authorization

HTTP/1.1 often needs multiple connections to avoid response blocking among requests. HTTP/2 multiplexes streams on a TCP connection, but transport loss can stall all dependent bytes. HTTP/3 uses QUIC streams and has different transport behavior. All share HTTP semantics at the application level. Protocol negotiation, proxies, payload sizes, and client implementations influence results. Do not infer that a protocol upgrade removes database time, server queues, or every form of head-of-line blocking. || Same resource semantics / HTTP/1.1 connection scheduling / HTTP/2 streams over TCP / HTTP/3 streams over QUIC

An existing connection avoids some setup work. A new TCP connection usually requires a transport handshake before normal traffic; secure connection establishment adds protocol-specific exchanges. Reuse connections with appropriate idle limits and connection-pool bounds. Excessively long lifetimes can retain unhealthy endpoints or stale DNS choices; excessively short ones waste CPU and round trips. The right lifetime depends on infrastructure timeouts, workload burstiness, and cancellation behavior rather than a universal keepalive number. || Cold request needs setup / Warm request reuses connection / Idle intermediaries may expire it / Pool validates or replaces connection

Round trips create a latency floor even before useful computation. In an illustrative cold path, DNS costs 20 ms, TCP 40 ms, TLS 40 ms, and request/response plus execution 55 ms: total 155 ms. This is a simplified sequential example, not a benchmark; resumption, QUIC, cached DNS, and overlap change it. Moving a service closer can reduce propagation delay, while batching or reuse reduces the number of required exchanges. || DNS: 20 ms / TCP setup: 40 ms / TLS setup: 40 ms / Request plus work: 55 ms

A forward proxy acts on behalf of clients; a reverse proxy fronts servers. A corporate forward proxy may control outbound access, while a reverse proxy terminates TLS, routes traffic, and enforces limits before an application. Both add another failure and timeout boundary. Configure trusted forwarding headers deliberately: accepting arbitrary client-supplied identity or source-address headers can bypass controls. Always identify which party selected the proxy and which upstream identity it can legitimately assert. || Client may choose forward proxy / Reverse proxy fronts application / Proxy forwards selected metadata / Application validates trusted boundary

A load balancer distributes traffic among backends; an API gateway commonly adds API-specific policy such as authentication integration, quotas, and request transformation. Products overlap, so compare responsibilities instead of names. A gateway that centralizes every business rule becomes a coupled deployment bottleneck. Keep domain authorization where resource ownership is known, and let an edge gateway perform appropriate coarse admission and routing. Neither component fixes an overloaded database by itself. || Edge receives request / Gateway applies boundary policy / Balancer chooses healthy backend / Domain service enforces resource rules

Layer-4 routing selects using transport information and may pass encrypted traffic through. Layer-7 routing understands application semantics such as HTTP host or path and generally needs access to those messages. L7 policy can be richer but consumes processing and creates parsing and trust obligations. A long-lived stream may remain attached to one backend even if later connections use another. Choose the level based on the routing decision, encryption placement, and connection behavior required. || L4: address and transport port / L7: host, path, headers / Select backend under policy / Existing stream retains affinity

Polling repeatedly asks for updates; server-sent events stream server-to-client events over HTTP; WebSockets support bidirectional messaging after protocol setup. With 1000 idle clients polling every five seconds, the baseline is 200 polls/s before useful updates. Streaming reduces redundant polls but holds connections and requires reconnect, heartbeat, buffering, and recovery design. Neither WebSockets nor SSE automatically supplies durable message delivery or application-level replay after a disconnected client returns. || 1000 clients / Poll every 5 seconds / Baseline: 200 requests/s / Streaming trades polls for connection state

NAT rewrites addressing, firewalls apply traffic policy, ingress enters a boundary, and egress leaves it. These functions can be implemented at different layers. An application timeout can result from an exhausted NAT table or a blocked return path, not only a slow server. Design outbound allowlists and connection lifetimes together. Network segmentation limits reachability but cannot replace per-request authentication and authorization, particularly when several tenants share a service network. || Outbound socket allocated / NAT mapping and firewall policy / Remote response returns / Expired state can break the path

Service discovery maps a logical service to reachable instances. It may use DNS, a registry, a platform control plane, or client configuration. Registrations and health information can lag reality. A client must handle stale endpoints and retry safely within a deadline. Cache discovery results to reduce control-plane dependence, but refresh them deliberately; a successful lookup is not proof the selected instance can serve the request or has capacity. || Instance registers / Discovery publishes endpoints / Client selects cached endpoint / Failure requires bounded refresh and retry

A CDN places cacheable representations near users; the origin remains responsible for authoritative content. Cache keys, freshness, authorization, and purge behavior determine correctness. Public thumbnails may tolerate stale copies, while personalized account pages require careful cache policy and partitioning. Edge delivery reduces some network and origin work but introduces another state copy. A cacheable error or omitted tenant dimension in a cache key can distribute the wrong result very efficiently. || Browser requests asset / Nearby edge checks cache key / Miss fetches origin / Cached representation has freshness policy

A timeout means the caller stopped waiting; it does not reveal whether the remote side executed. Packets may be delayed, dropped, reordered, or blocked while other paths remain healthy. Distinguish connection establishment, idle, and overall request deadlines. Retrying a non-idempotent action can duplicate effects; waiting indefinitely can exhaust resources. Treat partial failure as a normal execution history and reconcile ambiguous outcomes using stable operation identity or status queries. || Request may reach server / Server may commit / Reply may be lost / Caller sees only a timeout

A connection pool reuses a bounded set of connections. Borrowing can itself queue, so include acquisition in the deadline. A leak or oversized pool can exhaust both application workers and database capacity. Keepalive tests a transport path under particular conditions, not business correctness. In an assumed pool of twenty connections with 50 ms mean hold time, the ideal busy-capacity bound is 400 operations/s before overhead and variance; real safe load requires measurement. || Pool capacity: 20 / Mean hold time: 0.05 s / Ideal ceiling: 400 operations/s / Queue and timeout before exhaustion''')

put(4, '''A program is code; a process is an executing instance with an address space and resources; threads are execution contexts within a process. Threads commonly share heap memory while maintaining separate stacks and scheduling state. A process crash can terminate every thread regardless of source-module boundaries. Use processes when isolation or separate resource ownership matters; use threads when shared-address-space execution is appropriate. Neither choice alone makes shared application state race-free. || Program on disk / Process owns address space / Threads share process resources / Process failure removes its threads

A stack records call-local execution state; a heap stores dynamically managed objects under the runtime's allocation model. Lifetimes need not match lexical scope, particularly with closures, async tasks, and shared references. An upload buffer retained by an unfinished task can keep hundreds of megabytes live after its initiating request is gone. Explicitly bound ownership and release scarce external resources; garbage collection of memory does not guarantee timely closure of files or connections. || Request allocates buffer / Task retains reference / Request returns / Buffer remains until ownership ends

A scheduler decides which runnable execution context receives CPU time. Context switching saves and restores state and can disturb caches. More runnable threads may increase latency without increasing completed work when a fixed CPU is saturated. Blocking I/O can free CPU for another thread, whereas busy waiting consumes it. Investigate run-queue length, CPU utilization, blocking, and contention before selecting a thread count; one worker per request is an unbounded admission policy. || Runnable work queues / CPU runs one context / Context switch restores another / Too many runnable tasks add overhead

CPU-bound work spends its limiting time computing; I/O-bound work spends much of it waiting on external operations. For 1000 assumed uploads that each wait 100 ms for storage, sequential waiting takes about 100 seconds before other costs. Fifty concurrent waits have an ideal waiting-time total around two seconds, subject to bandwidth and provider limits. CPU-heavy compression instead needs actual compute parallelism or faster algorithms; asynchronous syntax alone does not create additional CPU capacity. || 1000 uploads / 100 ms assumed wait each / Sequential wait sum: 100 s / 50 concurrent waits: about 2 s ideal

Synchronous execution ties the caller's progress to completing an operation; asynchronous execution separates initiation from later completion. Blocking describes whether the executing thread waits; the two distinctions are related but not identical. An async function that calls a blocking filesystem operation can still stall an event-loop thread. Specify the entire path, including library and driver behavior. Prefer the simplest model that keeps deadlines and resource use bounded under the expected workload. || Start operation / Caller may await completion / Underlying call may still block / Inspect runtime and driver behavior

Nonblocking I/O reports readiness or completion without holding the thread throughout the wait. The event loop resumes a task when progress is possible. This helps large numbers of waiting connections but does not make every operation immediately complete. A loop that repeatedly polls without yielding is still wasteful. Use the platform's readiness or completion mechanism and isolate unavoidable blocking work in a bounded executor, with explicit cancellation and queue limits. || Submit I/O / Register readiness or completion / Run another ready task / Resume when operation can progress

Concurrency means activities can be in progress over overlapping periods; parallelism means execution actually occurs simultaneously. One core can interleave several tasks without running their instructions at the same instant. Concurrent code still has races because interleaving can occur between read and write operations. Parallelism adds hardware execution opportunities but not automatic correctness. Decide independently how much work may be in progress and how many resources may execute it at once. || Task A starts / Task A waits while B runs / Activities overlap in time / Multiple cores can execute simultaneously

An event loop runs ready callbacks or task continuations. A future or promise represents a result that may become available later; awaiting it registers a continuation or suspends a task according to the runtime. A callback that computes for two seconds can delay every other task on a single loop. Keep each turn bounded and make cancellation visible. Futures do not guarantee that work is durable, isolated, or successfully executed merely because it was scheduled. || Task awaits future / Loop runs ready work / Completion makes continuation ready / Long callback delays other tasks

A worker pool caps simultaneously executing jobs, while a bounded queue caps waiting jobs. With eight CPU workers and 1000 tasks, accepting every task into memory can still exhaust resources through queued payloads. Keep large upload bytes in object storage and queue compact references. Decide what happens when capacity is full: wait with a deadline, reject, or redirect work. A pool limits execution, not necessarily admission or total memory footprint. || Bounded input queue / 8 workers execute jobs / Queue stores object references / Full queue triggers admission policy

Shared memory allows direct access but needs synchronization; message passing transfers information between owners and requires protocol decisions. A single owner processing inventory commands sequentially can simplify local invariants. It can still fail, become a bottleneck, or replay a duplicate message after recovery. Passing a pointer to mutable shared state is not the same as transferring ownership. Pick the model based on state ownership and communication cost, not an assertion that one eliminates all concurrency problems. || Shared counter needs coordination / Owner receives commands / Owner updates private state / Messages can still repeat or fail

A read-modify-write sequence can lose updates when its steps interleave. Starting at zero, A reads zero, B reads zero, A writes one, and B writes one: two increments produced one. Protect the entire invariant, not only the final assignment. A critical section may use a lock or an appropriate atomic operation; separate process or database writers require coordination at their own shared boundary. A local mutex cannot protect another process's memory. || A reads 0 / B reads 0 / A writes 1 / B writes 1: increment lost

A mutex grants exclusive access; a semaphore limits concurrent permits; an atomic operation provides defined indivisibility and ordering for specified operations; a condition variable coordinates waiting for a predicate while releasing a lock. Recheck the predicate in a loop because wakeups do not guarantee it holds. Protect related values together if the invariant spans them. Replacing every field with an atomic does not automatically make a multi-field account transfer atomic. || Acquire lock / Check predicate in loop / Wait releases and reacquires lock / Change state and notify appropriately

Deadlock is a cycle of dependencies that prevents progress; starvation repeatedly denies a participant needed service; livelock keeps participants active without useful progress. A holds inventory then requests payment while B holds payment then requests inventory: neither can proceed. Use consistent lock ordering, narrower critical sections, or redesign ownership; timeouts need rollback and cleanup. A retry loop can convert deadlock into livelock unless it changes coordination or uses bounded randomized delay. || A holds inventory lock / B holds payment lock / A waits for payment / B waits for inventory: cycle

Visibility is not established by intuition about elapsed time. A writer's update and another thread's read need the ordering guarantees defined by the language and synchronization primitives. Happens-before is a formal relation used to reason about such guarantees, not a statement that every earlier wall-clock event is visible. Publish an immutable object through a synchronized channel or appropriate atomic reference. The Go memory model is one concrete specification; other languages must be checked separately. || Writer initializes object / Synchronizing publish operation / Reader synchronizes receive / Reader observes specified initialized state

Thread safety is a behavioral property under a stated concurrent-use contract. Immutable values avoid mutation races after safe publication, but references, initialization, and surrounding resources still need ownership rules. A frozen task record can be shared; a mutable cache mapping task IDs to records may still need synchronization. Avoid returning writable internal collections. Confinement, immutability, locks, and message ownership are complementary tools, with copying and coordination costs that depend on the workload. || Construct immutable value / Publish through defined boundary / Readers do not mutate value / Mutable container still needs coordination

Unlimited concurrency converts downstream slowness into memory and connection exhaustion. Backpressure bounds or slows producers according to available capacity. For an assumed worker rate of 100 jobs/s and arrivals of 150 jobs/s, a queue grows by fifty jobs/s until a limit is reached. A larger queue delays rejection but does not fix the rate mismatch. Prefer bounded queues, admission deadlines, compact payloads, and explicit overload responses; monitor queue age as well as length. || Arrivals: 150 jobs/s / Completion: 100 jobs/s / Backlog grows: 50 jobs/s / Bound queue and reject excess work

Cancellation must propagate to owned child work and release resources. Structured concurrency ties child-task lifetimes to a parent scope, so failures and completion have an explicit owner. Cancelling a caller does not prove an external side effect was undone: a payment may already have committed. Use finally-style cleanup for local resources and reconciliation for uncertain remote effects. Detached tasks need a durable owner when their business outcome must survive the request process. || Parent request starts children / Deadline cancels owned tasks / Cleanup releases local resources / Reconcile external effects separately''')
